.TH "ROTBYTE\-PERMISSIONS" "7" "2026\-07\-11" "rotbyte 1.3.0" "rotbyte Manual"
.SH NAME
rotbyte\-permissions \- grant macOS Full Disk Access for rotbyte scans
.SH DESCRIPTION
macOS restricts access to certain directories through a system called
TCC (Transparency, Consent, and Control).
rotbyte works fine from the terminal out of the box, but \f[B]scheduled
scans via \f[CB]\-\-track\f[B]\f[R] run in the background without a
terminal, so they need explicit permission.
.PP
This guide covers the one\-time setup to let rotbyte run unattended.
.RS
.PP
\f[B]Requires rotbyte v1.1.0 or later.\f[R] Earlier versions route
launchd through a bash wrapper, which breaks TCC attribution regardless
of FDA settings.
.RE
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS Do I need this?
\f[B]No\f[R] if you\(cqre only scanning directories that aren\(cqt
TCC\-protected, such as \f[CR]/data\f[R], \f[CR]/srv\f[R],
\f[CR]/tmp\f[R], or directories you created directly under
\f[CR]\(ti\f[R] (e.g.
\f[CR]\(ti/media\f[R]).
.PP
\f[B]Yes\f[R] if you\(cqre scanning any of these with
\f[CR]\-\-track\f[R]: \- \f[CR]\(ti/Desktop\f[R],
\f[CR]\(ti/Documents\f[R], \f[CR]\(ti/Downloads\f[R] \- External drives
(\f[CR]/Volumes/...\f[R]) \- Time Machine backups \- Any directory under
macOS privacy protection
.PP
If you only run rotbyte manually from the terminal, your terminal
app\(cqs existing FDA grant covers it.
The steps below are only needed for scheduled background scans.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS 1. Grant Full Disk Access to Python
When rotbyte runs via launchd, macOS checks whether the process
performing file I/O has Full Disk Access.
Since v0.1.1, rotbyte\(cqs launchd plists invoke Python directly
(skipping the Homebrew bash wrapper), so \f[B]Python is the only binary
that needs FDA\f[R].
.SS Shortcut: \f[CR]rotbyte \-\-grant\-fda\f[R]
Run this first \(em it checks whether Full Disk Access already looks
available and, if not, opens System Settings to the Full Disk Access
pane \f[B]and\f[R] reveals the correct Python binary in Finder, so you
only have to drag it in, toggle it on, and restart:
.IP
.EX
rotbyte \-\-grant\-fda
.EE
.PP
There\(cqs no macOS API to grant FDA or query its state directly, so the
check is a best\-effort probe \(em a \(lqnot granted\(rq result is
reliable, but a \(lqgranted\(rq result can reflect access inherited from
an already\-authorized terminal rather than a grant on the Python binary
itself, which won\(cqt help a launchd\-scheduled scan.
If scheduled scans still fail after \f[CR]\-\-grant\-fda\f[R] reports
success, follow the manual steps below and confirm with \(lqVerify it
works\(rq.
.SS Finding the right binary
The FDA file picker only accepts Mach\-O executables and \f[CR].app\f[R]
bundles \(em not scripts or symlinks.
Homebrew\(cqs \f[CR]python3\f[R] is a chain of symlinks, so you need the
real binary.
(\f[CR]\-\-grant\-fda\f[R] above finds this for you automatically.)
.PP
\f[B]Step 1: Find your Python version\f[R]
.IP
.EX
python3 \-\-version
.EE
.PP
\f[B]Step 2: Locate the Mach\-O binary\f[R]
.PP
Replace \f[CR]3.14\f[R] below with your actual version:
.IP
.EX
file /opt/homebrew/Cellar/python\(at3.14/*/Frameworks/Python.framework/Versions/3.14/Python
.EE
.PP
This should report \f[CR]Mach\-O 64\-bit executable\f[R].
That\(cqs the file you need.
.PP
If using Intel Mac, the path starts with \f[CR]/usr/local/Cellar/\f[R]
instead.
.PP
\f[B]Step 3: Add it in System Settings\f[R]
.IP "1." 3
Open \f[B]System Settings → Privacy & Security → Full Disk Access\f[R]
.IP "2." 3
Click \f[B]+\f[R] (unlock with your password if needed)
.IP "3." 3
Press \f[B]Cmd+Shift+G\f[R] in the file picker
.IP "4." 3
Paste the path to the \f[CR]Versions/3.XX/\f[R] directory
.IP "5." 3
Select the file named \f[CR]Python\f[R] (no extension)
.IP "6." 3
Toggle the switch \f[B]on\f[R]
.PP
Alternatively, look for \f[CR]Python.app\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
ls /opt/homebrew/Cellar/python\(at3.14/*/Frameworks/Python.framework/Versions/3.14/Resources/Python.app
.EE
.PP
If it exists, you can add that instead \(em \f[CR].app\f[R] bundles are
always accepted.
.PP
\f[B]Step 4: Restart your Mac\f[R]
.PP
TCC changes for launchd agents often don\(cqt take effect until after a
full reboot.
A \f[CR]launchctl unload/load\f[R] cycle is not sufficient.
.SS Verify it works
After restarting, regenerate the plists and test:
.IP
.EX
rotbyte \-\-track \(ti/Desktop/myfiles \-\-full\-at 2h
launchctl start com.rotbyte.full.HASH
sleep 3
cat /tmp/com.rotbyte.full.HASH.log
.EE
.PP
(Replace \f[CR]HASH\f[R] with the hash shown in the \f[CR]\-\-track\f[R]
output.)
.PP
A blank log means success \(em \f[CR]\-\-quiet\f[R] suppresses normal
output.
If you see \(lqauthorization denied,\(rq see Troubleshooting below.
.RS
.PP
\f[B]Note:\f[R] When Homebrew upgrades Python (e.g.\ 3.14 → 3.15), the
binary path changes and you\(cqll need to re\-add the new version to
Full Disk Access.
.RE
.SS After any \f[CR]brew upgrade\f[R]: run \f[CR]rotbyte \-\-repair\f[R]
Scheduled scans bake the interpreter and script path into their launchd
plists.
A Homebrew upgrade can delete those exact paths, and every scheduled run
then fails silently at launch (\f[CR]rotbyte \-\-status\f[R] shows
\f[CR]BROKEN ✗\f[R]).
After upgrading, run:
.IP
.EX
rotbyte \-\-repair
.EE
.PP
This re\-points every installed schedule at the current paths in place
\(em preserving all flags \(em and reloads it.
It\(cqs safe to run anytime; schedules already current are left
untouched.
(Re\-granting Full Disk Access, above, is still required separately when
the \f[I]Python\f[R] version itself changed.)
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS 2. Allow background items
Starting with macOS Ventura (13.0), the system notifies you when a new
background agent is installed.
When you run \f[CR]rotbyte \-\-track\f[R], macOS may show:
.RS
.PP
\f[B]\(lqrotbyte\(rq added items that can run in the background\f[R]
.RE
.PP
Open \f[B]System Settings → General → Login Items & Extensions\f[R] and
make sure the rotbyte entries are \f[B]allowed\f[R] (toggled on).
If you dismiss the notification without allowing, the scheduled scans
won\(cqt run.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS 3. Prevent sleep during overnight scans
Scheduled scans won\(cqt run if your Mac is asleep.
If you\(cqre running overnight full verifies
(e.g.\ \f[CR]\-\-full\-at 2h\f[R]):
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Desktops:\f[R] System Settings → Energy → enable \(lqPrevent
automatic sleeping when the display is off\(rq
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Laptops:\f[R] System Settings → Battery → Options → enable \(lqWake
for network access\(rq
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS 4. External volumes
External drives under \f[CR]/Volumes/\f[R] may need both Full Disk
Access and correct POSIX permissions.
If you see errors on an external drive:
.IP "1." 3
Ensure FDA is granted (step 1)
.IP "2." 3
Check ownership: \f[CR]ls \-la /Volumes/YourDrive/\f[R]
.IP "3." 3
If needed: \f[B]Finder → right\-click drive → Get Info → check
\(lqIgnore ownership on this volume\(rq\f[R]
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS 5. Confirm the right rotbyte is running
If you have rotbyte installed in multiple locations (e.g.\ a personal
copy in \f[CR]\(ti/.bin\f[R] and Homebrew in
\f[CR]/opt/homebrew/bin\f[R]), the plist will use whichever
\f[CR]which rotbyte\f[R] finds first.
Verify:
.IP
.EX
which rotbyte
.EE
.PP
This should return \f[CR]/opt/homebrew/bin/rotbyte\f[R].
If it returns a different path, either remove the other copy or use the
full Homebrew path when running \f[CR]\-\-track\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
/opt/homebrew/bin/rotbyte \-\-track \(ti/Desktop \-\-full\-at 2h
.EE
.PP
After running \f[CR]\-\-track\f[R], verify the plist calls Python
directly:
.IP
.EX
grep \-A5 ProgramArguments \(ti/Library/LaunchAgents/com.rotbyte.full.*.plist
.EE
.PP
You should see \f[CR]python3.14\f[R] and \f[CR]rotbyte.py\f[R] as
separate entries \(em \f[B]not\f[R] a single path to a bash wrapper
script.
.PP
